CAT = "phinsLifeLaw"; SEG = "LLW"
Q = [
("Under the Insurance Code, life insurance includes a contract to pay a sum if the insured:",
 "Survives a specified period", ["Wins a raffle prize", "Loses a business deal abroad", "Changes to a new employer"],
 "Section 182 says life insurance may be payable on death, on surviving a specified period, or otherwise on the continuance or cessation of life."),
("A contract for the payment of an annuity is treated under the Insurance Code as:",
 "A life insurance contract", ["A non-life insurance contract", "A bank deposit", "A pre-need plan"],
 "Sections 181 and 182 treat every contract for annuities and endowments as a life insurance contract for purposes of the Code."),
("As a general rule, who may change the beneficiary of a life policy?",
 "The insured", ["The beneficiary", "The agent", "The insurer"],
 "Section 11 gives the insured the right to change the beneficiary designated in the policy unless he has expressly waived that right."),
("A beneficiary named with the right to change expressly waived in the policy is:",
 "An irrevocable beneficiary", ["A revocable beneficiary", "A contingent beneficiary", "A tertiary beneficiary"],
 "When the insured waives the right to change in the policy, the designation is irrevocable and the beneficiary's consent is needed for changes that affect the beneficiary's interest."),
("Carlo named his brother as beneficiary and never changed it before he died. Under Section 11, the designation is:",
 "Deemed irrevocable", ["Void at his death", "Passed to his estate", "Open to the heirs"],
 "Section 11 provides that if the insured does not change the beneficiary during his lifetime, the designation is deemed irrevocable."),
("A beneficiary who is an accomplice in killing the insured:",
 "Forfeits her interest", ["Receives half the proceeds", "Is paid after a delay", "Keeps her share"],
 "Section 12 forfeits the interest of a beneficiary who is the principal, accomplice or accessory in willfully bringing about the insured's death."),
("When one of two beneficiaries forfeits his share for killing the insured, the share goes to:",
 "The other beneficiary", ["The insurer", "The insured's employer", "The agent who sold it"],
 "Under Section 12 the forfeited share passes to the other beneficiaries unless they are disqualified; failing them, it follows the policy or goes to the insured's estate."),
("Under Article 2012 of the Civil Code, who cannot be named beneficiary by a married insured?",
 "His paramour", ["His legitimate child", "His own sister", "His employer"],
 "A person forbidden to receive a donation under Article 739, such as one in an adulterous or concubinage relationship with the donor, cannot be named beneficiary."),
("Liza owns a policy on her son's life and dies first. Unless the policy says otherwise, ownership goes to:",
 "Her son, the insured", ["Liza's estate", "Liza's husband", "The insurer that issued it"],
 "Section 3 says rights in a policy taken out by an original owner on another's life automatically vest in the person insured on the original owner's death."),
("A minor beneficiary has no judicial guardian. Under Section 182, who may act for the minor first?",
 "The father", ["The insurance agent", "The family's landlord", "The barangay captain"],
 "Section 182 lets the father, or in his absence or incapacity the mother, exercise the minor's rights under the policy without court authority, within the limits the Code sets."),
("If neither parent can act for a minor beneficiary, Section 182 allows action by:",
 "An eldest sibling of 18", ["Any adult neighbor", "The insurer's own lawyer", "A younger sibling of 15"],
 "The grandparent, the eldest brother or sister at least 18, or any relative with actual custody may act as guardian without a court order."),
("The insurer is liable for suicide when it is committed:",
 "After the policy is two years old", ["At any time after the policy issues", "Only after five full years", "Only in the first policy year"],
 "Section 183 makes the insurer liable for suicide committed after the policy has been in force two years from issue or last reinstatement, unless the policy sets a shorter period."),
("An insured takes his own life while insane, a year after the policy was issued. The claim is:",
 "Compensable", ["Void", "Limited to premiums", "Payable after a year"],
 "Section 183 provides that suicide committed in a state of insanity is compensable regardless of the date of commission."),
("A policy that lapsed was reinstated. The two-year suicide period counts from:",
 "The last reinstatement", ["The original issue only", "The first claim", "The insured's birthday"],
 "Section 183 counts the period from the date of issue or of the last reinstatement, so reinstating a policy starts a new period."),
("Under Section 48, an insurer that wants to rescind a policy must do so before:",
 "An action on the policy begins", ["The first premium falls due", "The agent receives commission", "The policy is delivered"],
 "Section 48 says a right to rescind given to the insurer must be exercised previous to the commencement of an action on the contract. Its second paragraph adds the two-year incontestability rule for life policies."),
("The incontestability provision required by Section 233 does not stop the insurer from contesting for:",
 "Nonpayment of premium", ["A misstated occupation", "A forgotten illness", "An unclear answer"],
 "Section 233(b) excepts nonpayment of premium, and violation of conditions on military or naval service in time of war."),
("The grace period in an individual life policy is:",
 "30 days or one month", ["7 days or one week", "60 days or two months", "90 days or a quarter"],
 "Section 233(a) requires a grace period of either thirty days or one month for any premium after the first, during which the policy stays in force."),
("The grace period applies to:",
 "Premiums after the first", ["The first premium only", "Every premium, first included", "Only single premium plans"],
 "Section 233(a) gives the grace period for the payment of any premium after the first; cover does not begin until the first premium is paid."),
("Bea dies during the grace period with her premium unpaid. The insurer:",
 "Pays, less the unpaid premium", ["Pays nothing at all", "Pays only the premiums paid in", "Pays double the face amount"],
 "Under Section 233(a), the policy stays in force during grace, but the overdue premium, with any interest, may be deducted from the amount payable."),
("Under Section 233, a policy and an attached copy of the application form:",
 "The entire contract", ["Two separate contracts", "A temporary contract", "A contract with the agent"],
 "Section 233(c) requires a provision that the policy, with the application if attached, constitutes the entire contract between the parties."),
("Section 233's misstatement of age provision applies when age is used to set:",
 "The premium and benefits", ["The beneficiary's share", "The agent's commission", "The grace period length"],
 "Section 233(d) adjusts the amount payable to what the premium would have bought at the correct age, so it matters only where age determines premium and benefits."),
("A lapsed policy may be reinstated within how long of the default?",
 "Three years", ["Six months", "One year", "Five full years"],
 "Section 233(j) allows reinstatement within three years from default unless the cash surrender value was paid or the extension period expired."),
("Which does Section 233 require to reinstate a lapsed policy?",
 "Evidence of insurability", ["A new beneficiary named", "A court order", "A new agent's endorsement"],
 "The policy owner must give satisfactory evidence of insurability and pay all overdue premiums and indebtedness, with interest."),
("A policy cannot be reinstated under Section 233 if:",
 "The cash value was paid out", ["The owner has moved abroad", "The selling agent resigned", "A rider was added later"],
 "Reinstatement is not available once the cash surrender value has been duly paid or the extended term period has expired."),
("A life policy may be transferred by will to someone without insurable interest. That person:",
 "May recover on it", ["Cannot recover on it", "Gets only premiums back", "Needs court consent"],
 "Section 184 lets a life or health policy pass by transfer, will or succession to any person, whether or not with insurable interest."),
("Is notice to the insurer needed to keep a transferred life policy valid?",
 "Only if the policy requires it", ["Always, within thirty days", "Never, in any case", "Only for policies over a set size"],
 "Section 185 says notice of a transfer or bequest is not necessary to preserve validity unless the policy expressly requires it."),
("When a life policy matures by death, the insurer must pay within:",
 "60 days of proof of death", ["15 days after the funeral", "6 months of the claim", "1 year after the death"],
 "Section 248 requires death proceeds to be paid within sixty days after presentation of the claim and filing of proof of death."),
("An endowment matures. When must the insurer pay?",
 "Immediately on maturity", ["Within 90 days", "After one full year", "Whenever it chooses to"],
 "Section 248 says proceeds are paid immediately upon maturity unless payable in installments or as an annuity."),
("If the insurer delays a death claim without a fraud defense, the beneficiary may collect:",
 "Interest for the delay", ["The agent's commission", "A second face amount", "Nothing beyond the claim"],
 "Section 248 entitles the beneficiary to interest at twice the ceiling prescribed by the Monetary Board for the duration of the delay, unless the claim is refused as fraudulent."),
("A beneficiary submits a fake death certificate to claim. Under Section 251, this is:",
 "Unlawful", ["A minor irregularity", "Allowed if repaid", "A matter for the agent"],
 "Section 251 makes it unlawful to present a fraudulent claim or prepare a writing to support one, punishable by fine, imprisonment or both."),
("Which is an unfair claim settlement practice under Section 247?",
 "Misstating policy terms", ["Asking for proof of death", "Paying within 60 days", "Checking a claimant's ID"],
 "Knowingly misrepresenting pertinent facts or policy provisions to claimants, done as a general practice, is listed in Section 247."),
("A person who takes an absolute assignment of a life policy becomes:",
 "The new policy owner", ["A contingent beneficiary", "The person insured", "A co-insurer of it"],
 "An absolute assignment transfers all ownership rights. A collateral assignment only secures a debt and leaves ownership with the assignor."),
]
